.TH BYTECODE\-INSPECT 1 2026-10-25 "blocktools 0.1.0" "blocktools manual"
.SH NAME
bytecode\-inspect \- classify runtime bytecode (EIP\-1167 proxy, EIP\-7702 delegation, EOF, plain code),
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
bytecode\-inspect [HEX]
HEX (or stdin): runtime code as 0x\-hex, or an eth_getCode JSON\-RPC response ({"result": "0x.."}) as\-is.
Prints kind (empty, code, eip1167_proxy + implementation, eip7702_delegation + delegate, eof), size,
exceeds_eip170, keccak256 (the code hash), metadata (solc version, ipfs/bzzr hash), opcode counts and
selector_candidates: PUSH4 values compared with EQ (heuristic; confirm with sig\-hash \-\-lookup).
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
check the EIP\-170 size limit, parse the Solidity CBOR metadata trailer, count notable opcodes
and list dispatcher selector candidates (heuristic), offline.
.nf
.RS 2
  jq \-r .result getCode.json | bytecode\-inspect
  bytecode\-inspect < eth_getCode\-response.json
.RE
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.nf
\-\-lookup
.fi
Each flag's meaning is in the SYNOPSIS usage line(s) above (taken from \-\-help).
.SH INPUT/OUTPUT
.nf
.RS 2
  jq \-r .result getCode.json | bytecode\-inspect
  bytecode\-inspect < eth_getCode\-response.json
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Measured on Linux by the EXAMPLES below (scripts/gen\-man.py runs them):
.TP
0
last status ok
.TP
2
last status bad_input
.PP
Not every tool uses the same code for bad input: some exit 1, others exit 2 (docs/FINDINGS.md D1\-14). Always read the last JSON status line. Status and failure codes: docs/REFUSALS.md.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ bytecode\-inspect 0x363d3d373d3d3d363d73bebebebebebebebebebebebebebebebebebebebe5af43d82803e903d91602b57fd5bf3
# exit 0, last status: ok
$ bytecode\-inspect 0x1
# exit 2, last status: bad_input
.fi
